itle=3D"Edit section: Possessions"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-e=
ditsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id=3D"mwVg">Lord Lovelace had three homes: <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ockham_Park" title=3D"Ockham Park" id=3D"=
mwVw">Ockham Park</a>, Surrey; Ben Damph Estate on <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torridon" title=3D"Torridon" id=3D"mwW=
A">Loch Torridon</a> in <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Ross-shire" title=3D"Ross-shire" id=3D"mwWQ">Ross-shire</a>; and =
a house in London.</p>

<section data-mw-section-id=3D"6" id=3D"mwWg" aria-labelledby=3D"Horsley_To=
wers"><div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Horsley_Towers">Horsl=
ey Towers</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-=
bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWi=
lliam_King-Noel,_1st_Earl_of_Lovelace&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D6" ti=
tle=3D"Edit section: Horsley Towers"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw=
-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id=3D"mwWw">Lord Lovelace acquired <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horsley_Towers" title=3D"Horsley Towers" id=3D"mwXA=
">Horsley Towers</a> (now a hotel) in <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Horsley" title=3D"East Horsley" id=3D"mwXQ">Ea=
st Horsley</a> and was patron of the <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parish_church" title=3D"Parish church" id=3D"mwXg">p=
arish church</a> funding the rebuilding of the chancel and the nave in 1869=
.<sup about=3D"#mwt23" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_ref-6" rel=3D"=
dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;name&quot;:&qu=
ot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw=
-reference-text-cite_note-6&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/William_King-Noel,_1st_Earl_of_Lovelace#cite_note-6" id=3D"mwXw"><span c=
lass=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwYA"><span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwY=
Q">[</span>6<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwYg">]</span></span></a></s=
up> He also rebuilt the wall of the churchyard which included a number of a=
rchitectural features such as the gazebo and its family crest-engraved wall=
s. He planned for his death 20 years before he died when he began work on a=
 mausoleum in another corner of the churchyard. This mausoleum has recently=
 been restored.<sup about=3D"#mwt24" class=3D"mw-ref reference" id=3D"cite_=
ref-7" rel=3D"dc:references" typeof=3D"mw:Extension/ref" data-mw=3D"{&quot;=
name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&=
quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-7&quot;}}"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/William_King-Noel,_1st_Earl_of_Lovelace#cite_note-7" id=3D=
"mwYw"><span class=3D"mw-reflink-text" id=3D"mwZA"><span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket" id=3D"mwZQ">[</span>7<span class=3D"cite-bracket" id=3D"mwZg">]</span>=
</span></a></sup></p>

<p id=3D"mwZw">He had fifteen bridges, known as the <a rel=3D"mw:WikiLink" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lovelace_Bridges" title=3D"Lovelace B=
ridges" id=3D"mwaA">Lovelace Bridges</a>, constructed on his estate in <a r=
el=3D"mw:WikiLink" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Horsley" titl=
e=3D"East Horsley" id=3D"mwaQ">East Horsley</a> to facilitate the transport=
 of timber by horse-drawn carts. The bridges were built where the tracks cr=
ossed existing bridleways or roads. Ten bridges still exist.</p>

